{"reference":"2 Corinthians 7","book":"2 Corinthians","chapter":7,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Paul describes a relationship tested by a painful earlier letter and then renewed through Titus’s report. The letter and conflict are strongly implied, but their details remain uncertain; the unnamed wrongdoer and injured party should not be confidently identified with a particular case elsewhere.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 7:5-16","2 Corinthians 7:8-12","2 Corinthians 7:12"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_007_383150d1dcb26991c5bc85f9"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Grief and Repentance","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Paul does not celebrate sorrow for its own sake. He distinguishes grief that moves toward repentance and repair from grief that leads toward death. The passage therefore evaluates grief by its direction and outcome, rather than by emotional intensity, public humiliation, self-hatred, or compliance with an authority figure.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 7:5-16","2 Corinthians 7:8-12"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_007_010fb0862f5019064ede256f"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"An Openhearted Appeal","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Paul’s appeal to “open your hearts” is relational rhetoric: he asks the Corinthians to receive him while defending himself against charges of wrongdoing, corruption, and exploitation. Such language describes invited reciprocity; it does not authorize leaders to demand emotional access, loyalty, disclosure, affection, or reconciliation from people under their authority.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 7:2-4"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_007_8f0b49482cbb62b787aef69a"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Care in Conflict","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Because Paul later distinguishes fruitful grief from destructive grief, this chapter cannot make distress, shame, panic, or trauma an inherently godly method of correction. Privacy can protect people and fair process, but secrecy that hides danger, blocks reporting, destroys records, or shields reputation is concealment rather than confidentiality.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 7:5-13","2 Corinthians 7:5-16"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_007_e86c7017fb16f56c2b1816ba"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_5","text":"Titus’s arrival and report reshape Paul’s understanding of the Corinthians’ response. The narrative makes mediated reporting important, while also cautioning that one envoy’s account should not automatically be treated as complete without independent corroboration.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 7:5-16"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_007_fb049e1741609d16c9ca13ab"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Passage Before Chapter 7","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"Verse 1 completes the purity appeal of 6:14–18. The preceding “unequal yoking” language may concern idolatrous association, opponents, communal boundaries, or a separate letter unit; its precise reference and modern scope remain disputed. It does not directly name interracial or interfaith marriage, business contracts, or friendship, and its temple and purity language cannot authorize treating other people as contamination.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 6:14-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_007_7704e7da25d1bb2cb2ea5c29"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}